' The C64 one-liner, on the board.
'
' 10 PRINT CHR$(205.5+RND(1)); : GOTO 10 was the whole program: two
' diagonal characters picked at random, filling the screen with a maze
' nobody designed. The diagonals sit at 215 and 216 in this font, and
' the rest is unchanged - a label, a print, a jump.
'
' The semicolon is doing half the work. Without it every character gets
' its own line and the maze is a single column down the left edge. And
' RND wants its parentheses: inside a FUNC or SUB a bare RND is an
' undeclared local rather than the builtin, which makes it zero, which
' makes every character the same one.
'
' The array form reads better and does not fit here:
'
'   PRINT JOIN(CHR$(215 + INT(RND(IOTA(1160, 0)) * 2)), "");
'
' Every operator in an array expression materialises a whole new array,
' so that chain is five arrays of 1160 elements at 24 bytes each against
' the seventy kilobytes this board has free. The jump below does the
' same work in no memory at all.

Lo:
PRINT CHR$(215 + INT(RND()*2));
GOTO Lo
